# Forbidden patterns for secret / credential leakage.
# Applied to all tracked text files (no extension filter).
# Format: [(?-i)]<regex><TAB><description>   (one per line; # comments ignored)
# Copy to your project as `.forbidden-patterns/secrets.txt`.
#
# All patterns are ERE-compatible (no PCRE lookaheads).
# Separator is TAB — patterns can contain literal `|` for ERE alternation.
# In character classes, place `-` at the start or end to keep it literal
# (BSD grep rejects `\-`). Prefix patterns are high-specificity to keep the
# false-positive rate low.
#
# CASE SENSITIVITY. Rules match case-insensitively unless prefixed with the
# literal marker `(?-i)`, which check-secrets strips and turns into a
# case-sensitive grep. (It is a scaffold convention, not ERE syntax — POSIX
# ERE has no inline flags, so never pass `(?-i)` to grep yourself.)
#
# Token-shaped rules below carry the marker because vendor key formats are
# case-sensitive by specification: folding case cannot add a true positive,
# only false ones. The AWS `ACCA` prefix and the Twilio `AC` SID prefix are
# both composed entirely of hex characters, so case-folded they match inside
# ordinary SHA-256 digests — enough to fail any repo with a lockfile, over
# content that holds no credential at all. Base64-shaped rules (JWT `eyJ`,
# the Bedrock and PyPI prefixes) and the uppercase PEM banner are likewise
# meaningless when case-folded.
#
# The keyword-shaped rules (`password =`, `secret_key`) and the URL-scheme
# rules deliberately OMIT the marker: those match human-written prose and
# identifiers where case genuinely varies.

# Cloud / SaaS tokens — prefix-specific, very low false-positive rate
(?-i)(AKIA|ASIA|ABIA|ACCA)[0-9A-Z]{16}	AWS access key ID (AKIA/ABIA/ACCA) or temporary session key (ASIA) — rotate immediately
(?-i)AIza[0-9A-Za-z_-]{35}	Google API key — rotate immediately
(?-i)gh[pousr]_[A-Za-z0-9]{36,}	GitHub token — rotate immediately
(?-i)github_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_]{22,}	GitHub fine-grained personal access token — rotate immediately
(?-i)xox[baprs]-[A-Za-z0-9-]{10,}	Slack token — rotate immediately
(?-i)xapp-[0-9]-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{10,}	Slack app-level token (Socket Mode) — rotate immediately
(?-i)xoxe-[0-9]-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}	Slack config/refresh token — rotate immediately
(?-i)sk-[A-Za-z0-9]{48,}	Legacy OpenAI-style API key — rotate immediately
(?-i)sk-ant-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}	Anthropic API key (sk-ant-) — rotate immediately
(?-i)sk-proj-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}	OpenAI project API key (sk-proj-) — rotate immediately
(?-i)sk-(svcacct|admin)-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}	OpenAI service-account/admin key — rotate immediately
(?-i)hf_[A-Za-z0-9]{34,}	Hugging Face access token — rotate immediately
(?-i)glpat-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}	GitLab personal access token — rotate immediately
(?-i)npm_[A-Za-z0-9]{36}	npm access token — rotate immediately
(?-i)dckr_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}	Docker Hub personal access token (dckr_pat_) — rotate immediately
(?-i)pypi-AgEIcHlwaS5vcmc[A-Za-z0-9_-]{50,}	PyPI upload token — rotate immediately
(?-i)(sk|rk)_(live|prod)_[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}	Stripe live/restricted key — rotate immediately
(?-i)whsec_[A-Za-z0-9]{24,}	Stripe webhook signing secret (whsec_) — lets an attacker forge signed events; rotate immediately
hooks\.slack\.com/(services|workflows|triggers)/[A-Za-z0-9+/_-]{20,}	Slack webhook URL — rotate immediately
(?-i)dop_v1_[a-f0-9]{64}	DigitalOcean personal access token — rotate immediately
(?-i)(^|[^A-Za-z0-9])dapi[a-f0-9]{32}	Databricks API token — rotate immediately
(?-i)pplx-[A-Za-z0-9]{48}	Perplexity API key — rotate immediately
(?-i)ABSKQmVkcm9ja0FQSUtleS[A-Za-z0-9+/]{40,}={0,2}	AWS Bedrock long-term API key — rotate immediately
(?-i)sb_secret_[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}	Supabase secret API key (bypasses RLS) — rotate immediately
(?-i)sk-or-v1-[a-f0-9]{40,}	OpenRouter API key — rotate immediately
(?-i)gl(oas|dt|rtr?|ptt|agent|soat|ffct|imt|ft|wt)-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}	GitLab token (OAuth/deploy/runner/trigger/agent/SCIM/feed/etc.) — rotate immediately

# JWT (two base64url segments each starting with eyJ, the encoding of `{"`).
# Structural signature of a real header.payload pair — random base64 doesn't
# decode to {" twice. The header segment is always long, so it keeps the {17,}
# floor; the payload floor is only {2,} because a compact claim set (`{"id":7}`
# → eyJpZCI6N30) is short yet still a valid, leakable signed token. Expired
# demo/test tokens in docs use `scaffold-allow`.
(?-i)eyJ[A-Za-z0-9_-]{17,}\.eyJ[A-Za-z0-9_-]{2,}	JWT in source — if long-lived (service key), rotate; use env vars

# Private keys of any flavor (RSA / EC / DSA / OPENSSH / bare)
(?-i)-----BEGIN [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY-----	Private key in source — rotate and move to a secret manager

# URL with embedded credentials — e.g. protocol scheme + userinfo + host.
# `[a-zA-Z]+` rather than `[a-z]+` so the pattern reads correctly when copied
# into other tooling that doesn't run grep with -i.
[a-zA-Z]+://[^[:space:]:/@]*:[^[:space:]/@]+@[^[:space:]/]+	URL with embedded credentials — use env vars

# Hardcoded credentials in assignments. Value must be 16+ chars of token-like
# content to dodge test fixtures / short placeholders. Six per-keyword lines
# collapsed into one alternation now that the separator is TAB.
#
# The keyword must sit IMMEDIATELY before the separator, which is what let
# `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY = "..."` through (#87): `secret` matched, then the next
# required token was `_ACCESS_KEY`, not `=`. The AKIA rule above covers the AWS
# access key ID; nothing covered the paired SECRET, which is the dangerous half,
# and that spelling is the canonical one in every AWS SDK and CI config. The
# secret* alternatives are ordered longest-first so the rule behaves the same in
# a leftmost-FIRST engine (PCRE, and anything this file gets copied into) as it
# does under POSIX leftmost-longest grep -E.
(^|[^A-Za-z])(password|passwd|pwd|secret[-_]?access[-_]?key|secret[-_]?key|secret|private[-_]?key|credentials?|token|api[-_]?key|access[-_]?token)[[:space:]]*[:=][[:space:]]*['"][A-Za-z0-9_/+=-]{16,}['"]	Hardcoded credential — use env vars or a secret manager

# More cloud / SaaS prefixes (2026-06-30 audit) — prefix/boundary-anchored, validated against an FP corpus (SHA/UUID/lockfile-hash negatives)
(?-i)SG\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]{22}\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]{43}	SendGrid API key — rotate immediately
(?-i)shp(at|ca|pa|ss)_[a-fA-F0-9]{32}	Shopify access token — rotate immediately
(?-i)sq0(atp|csp)-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{22,}	Square OAuth/access token — rotate immediately
(?-i)(^|[^A-Za-z0-9])key-[a-f0-9]{32}	Mailgun API key — rotate immediately
(?-i)[0-9]{8,10}:AA[A-Za-z0-9_-]{33}	Telegram bot token — rotate immediately
(?-i)(^|[^A-Za-z0-9])AC[a-f0-9]{32}([^a-fA-F0-9]|$)	Twilio Account SID — rotate the paired auth token
(?-i)(^|[^A-Za-z0-9])SK[a-f0-9]{32}([^a-fA-F0-9]|$)	Twilio API key SID — rotate immediately

# Azure (2026-09 audit) — the one major cloud with no prefix rule here, while AWS,
# Google, GitHub, GitLab, Slack, Stripe and Twilio all have one. Neither shape can
# reach the generic keyword rule above: a connection string / dotenv line is
# unquoted, and its keyword is `AccountKey`, which the alternation does not carry.
# A Storage account key is unscoped, non-expiring read/write/delete over the whole
# account. NOTE: Azurite's well-known dev-storage key has exactly this shape and is
# a published constant, not a credential — mark that line `scaffold-allow`.
(?-i)AccountKey=[A-Za-z0-9+/]{86}==	Azure Storage account key (connection string) — full account access; rotate immediately
(?-i)(^|[^A-Za-z0-9])[A-Za-z0-9_~.]{3}8Q~[A-Za-z0-9_~.-]{34}([^A-Za-z0-9_~.-]|$)	Azure AD / Entra app client secret — rotate immediately

# Three more formats that matched no rule as of the same audit. The npm one
# matters because the scaffold itself installs a tracked `.npmrc` (--npm-cooldown)
# and check-filenames does not block that file; the existing `npm_` rule covers
# only the modern granular token, not the legacy `_authToken=<uuid>` CI form.
# `_authToken` is a config KEY, not a vendor token shape, so it omits the `(?-i)`
# marker (case genuinely varies) — an env reference such as
# `_authToken=${NPM_TOKEN}` does not match, since `$` is outside the value class.
# It is anchored to npmrc SYNTAX: line start, or the `/`/`:` that ends the
# registry URL (`//registry.npmjs.org/:_authToken=...`). check-secrets scans every
# tracked text file with no extension filter and this rule is case-insensitive, so
# without that anchor it fired on ordinary source that merely has a field of the
# same name — `this._authToken = incomingAuthToken;`,
# `self._authToken = incoming_auth_token`, and even
# `const _authToken = process.env.NPM_TOKEN ?? "";`, which is the remediation this
# rule's own message prescribes. `.` is out of the value class for the same reason
# (a dotted expression like `response.access_token` cannot reach the 16-char floor).
(?-i)GOCSPX-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}	Google OAuth client secret — rotate immediately
(^|[/:])_authToken[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*["']?[A-Za-z0-9_~+/-]{16,}	npm auth token in an .npmrc — publish-capable; rotate and read it from NPM_TOKEN instead
(?-i)hv[sbr]\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]{24,}	HashiCorp Vault service/batch/recovery token — rotate immediately
